From d01fa2eff20f1a5a8d3cc6461ac546e5632229d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iasnezhkov <iasnezhkov@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:55:10 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] v4l2: negotiate the driver's 10-bit tile CAPTURE format and
 detile it to P010

mtk_vcodec_dec_v1 does not accept a plain V4L2_PIX_FMT_P010 CAPTURE
request for a Main10 HEVC stream: V4L2Queue::setFormat() requires the
ioctl to echo back the exact fourcc it was given, and TRY_FMT/S_FMT
instead coerce the pixelformat field to the driver's own MT21S10T
('MTST') tile block layout, so setFormat() returns nullopt and
setupOutputFormat() falls through every entry in kSupportedOutputFourccs
without success - decode of a 10-bit stream currently fails outright
before a single CAPTURE buffer is queued. This is the same silent
substitution patch 0005 found for the 8-bit NV12 request (-> MM21
tiles), except here the driver is honest about it in the returned
pixelformat instead of hiding it.

Add kMt21S10TFourcc/kMt21S10RFourcc (v4l2_fourcc('M','T','S','T') and
('M','T','S','R')) to kSupportedOutputFourccs so the negotiation loop
requests the tile format directly and setFormat's echo check passes.
startOutputQueue() records which one was negotiated in the new
mCaptureIsMt10/mCaptureMt10Raster members; changeResolution() treats
both the same as P010 for HalPixelFormat pool selection; the DQBUF
path in serviceDeviceTask() branches to build a TiledMT10Image and
call the new VideoFrame::detileFrom10() instead of the existing
TiledMM21Image/detileFrom() 8-bit path.

Layout: tools/rigs/mt10conv.c in the main repo works out the exact
byte layout of MT21S10T/MT21S10R and is the source this patch's
decoder is ported from. Two facts anchor it: the upstream kernel
documents V4L2_PIX_FMT_MT2110T/MT2110R (Documentation/userspace-api/
media/v4l/pixfmt-yuv-planar.rst) as 16x32 luma / 16x16 chroma tiles,
640/320 bytes each, split into 8/4 partitions of 80 bytes (16 LSB +
64 MSB, LSB either tiled-by-column or raster-by-row); and the real
device's negotiated MTST sizeimage for 1920x1088 (3916800 bytes,
task brief 23.09, before the kernel Main10 sizeimage fix) matches
this tile math to the byte with zero slack, which also proves the Y
region is immediately followed by the UV region with no padding in
the driver's single-plane MT21S10T buffer. The one unproven piece is
the exact bit order within an LSB byte (which row/column occupies
which of the four 2-bit fields) - inferred from the doc's prose, not
yet checked against real decoded pixels. tools/rigs/mt10conv.c's -t
self-test round-trips a synthetic pattern through encode->decode for
both tile and raster modes and matches exactly (0/N mismatches, C and
NEON paths byte-identical, NEON ~14-15x faster) - this proves the
implementation is internally consistent, not that it matches the real
hardware's bit order.

ImageConverter gains TiledMT10Image/P010WritableImage and a
convertMT10ToP010() method that defaults to returning false, so
MdpImageConverter needs no changes and 10-bit always takes the CPU
path - the MDP hardware converter almost certainly has no support for
this format and wiring a zero-copy gralloc attempt for it is left for
later. LibyuvImageConverter implements convertMT10ToP010() by hand
(libyuv has no MT2110T/MTST equivalent): the same per-tile decode
tools/rigs/mt10conv.c uses, in plain-C form only - the NEON path
proven in the rig is not ported into this HAL code yet, left for a
follow-up once the layout itself is confirmed on-device.

Not done / not verified: no m/mm build (not allowed for this task),
no device test, no NEON in the HAL path, no MT2110T/MT2110R
(non-contiguous two-plane) variant since the device negotiates the
single-plane MT21S10T/MT21S10R family. The real validation step is a
raw MTST dump from the device compared against an ffmpeg P010
reference with tools/rigs/mt10conv.c -c; see the task report for the
exact dump this needs.

